# SaaS Platform Security (multi-tenant control plane)
Accordex Systems Pvt Ltd / Softmerce Technologies

These rules EXTEND `10-security.mdc`; they do not replace it. Where the base file
gives a general principle (e.g. object-level authorization), the rules here state
how it MUST be applied in this specific platform. If any instruction conflicts,
the stricter rule wins. Mark enforcement points in code with a `SECURITY:` comment.

This platform sells software products to client companies (tenants). It uses
**database-per-tenant**: a shared control-plane database, plus one separate
database per tenant. The biggest risks are (1) one tenant reaching another
tenant's data, (2) a user exceeding what their plan/role allows, and (3) billing
tampering or double-charging. The rules below exist to prevent exactly those.

## 1. Tenant isolation (the #1 risk in this system)
- The tenant identity for any request MUST be derived from the authenticated
  session/token on the server — NEVER from a client-supplied tenant id, body
  field, query param, header, or hostname the user can set.
- A request MUST NEVER be able to choose, name, or influence WHICH tenant
  database it connects to. The database target is resolved server-side from the
  authenticated tenant only.
- Every query against tenant data must be scoped to the resolved tenant. There is
  no "all tenants" query path in normal request handling. Cross-tenant reads are
  reserved for explicit platform-admin operations that are separately authorized
  and audited.
- `SECURITY:` comment required at the point where tenant identity is resolved and
  where the tenant database connection is selected.

## 2. Cross-tenant query guard (fail closed)
- Provide a data-access guard (e.g. Prisma middleware or a repository layer) that
  refuses to run a tenant-scoped query when no tenant scope is set. Missing scope
  = throw, never "return everything".
- Default-deny applies to data access, not just permissions: if the code cannot
  prove which tenant a query belongs to, it must fail, not guess.

## 3. Tenant database credentials & the connection manager
- Tenant DB credentials (host, user, password) are stored ENCRYPTED at rest
  (AES-256-GCM or equivalent), key from env/secrets manager — never in source,
  never in plain columns.
- Decryption happens ONLY inside the connection manager at the moment a
  connection is opened. Decrypted credentials must never be logged, returned in
  an API response, included in an error message, or placed in client-visible state.
- Each tenant database uses a LEAST-PRIVILEGE database user scoped to that
  database only. Never connect tenants using a shared superuser or a user that can
  see other databases.
- The connection manager must cap concurrent tenant connections and evict idle
  ones; a tenant must not be able to exhaust connections for others
  (denial-of-service protection).

## 4. Two-layer authorization (plan ceiling + user/role grant)
- Every protected action is checked on the SERVER against BOTH layers:
  (a) the tenant's plan ceiling for that product/plan/industry, AND
  (b) the user's effective grant (role grant, with per-user override winning).
  Effective permission = planCeiling AND (userOverride ?? roleGrant).
- A node/action not permitted by the plan can NEVER be allowed by a role or user
  grant. The plan is a hard ceiling.
- Authorization is enforced in the API/server layer. UI hiding of buttons or
  fields is UX only and is NEVER the security boundary.
- Missing or unresolved permission = deny (fail closed). If a stored grant exceeds
  the current plan ceiling (e.g. after a downgrade), the action is denied and the
  event is logged.
- `SECURITY:` comment required at each server-side permission check.

## 5. Usage-limit & metering integrity (gates revenue)
- Usage checks that gate actions (especially seat / pay-to-expand limits and any
  hard-block service) are enforced on the SERVER against the authoritative
  control-plane balance — never trusted from the client and never enforced in the
  UI alone.
- Limit increases happen only as a result of a verified event (a confirmed
  payment, or an explicit admin action), never from a client request asserting a
  new limit.
- Counting writes from tenant databases into the authoritative ledger must be
  idempotent so retries cannot inflate or deflate usage.

## 6. Billing, payments & webhooks
- NEVER store raw card data (PAN, CVV). Use the payment gateway's vault/token;
  store only gateway references.
- Verify the cryptographic signature of every inbound payment/gateway webhook
  before acting on it; reject unsigned or invalid-signature payloads.
- All payment and renewal operations are idempotent (idempotency keys / unique
  constraints) so a retried webhook or job can NEVER double-charge or
  double-credit.
- Money is stored in minor units (integers); never use floating point for
  currency. Freeze currency + FX rate onto an invoice at issue time.
- Invoice numbering (Series) must be allocated atomically under concurrency so two
  invoices can never share a number.

## 7. Tenant provisioning & lifecycle safety
- Provisioning a tenant database runs as a background job with least-privilege
  rights; it must be idempotent and must clean up partial state on failure
  (no half-provisioned tenant left reachable).
- On suspension/cancellation, access is revoked server-side immediately; the
  tenant database retention/deletion follows the documented policy and the action
  is audited.
- Integration tokens / API keys issued to products or tenants are stored encrypted,
  shown once, and revocable. Treat them like passwords.

## 8. Platform-admin (super) actions
- Platform-admin actions that can cross tenant boundaries (impersonation, support
  access, cross-tenant reports) require explicit elevated authorization, are
  logged to the append-only event log with actor + target tenant, and are
  time-boxed where possible.
- "Log in as tenant" / impersonation must be clearly recorded and must not silently
  inherit super privileges inside the tenant beyond what the support task needs.

## 9. Audit (non-negotiable for a billing system)
- Write an append-only event-log entry for: auth success/failure, permission
  denials, plan/role/privilege changes, plan-ceiling violations, usage-limit
  blocks and overrides, every invoice/payment/refund, provisioning/suspension, and
  every platform-admin cross-tenant action.
- The event log records actor, tenant, action, and before/after where relevant.
  It is never editable or deletable through application code.

> These rules are specific to this platform. General secure-coding requirements
> (input validation, injection prevention, output encoding, secrets, transport,
> error non-leakage, dependencies) remain governed by `10-security.mdc` and apply
> in full.
